      "title": "Hist2Pheno enables nuclei-resolved phenotypic prediction from histological images in esophageal squamous cell carcinoma",
      "presenter": "Lingyu Li",
      "format": "In person",
      "authors": "Lingyu Li, Lu Yu, Yuanhua Huang",
      "abstract": "Cellular phenotypes within the tumor immune microenvironment are critical for understanding tumor progression and response to immune checkpoint inhibitors, yet are largely shaped by spatial tumor- immune colocalization[1]. Spatial profiling technologies such as CODEX[2] or PhenoCycler Fusion (PCF) enable high-dimensional, spatially resolved characterization of cell states, but are limited by cost, throughput and scalability. In contrast, haematoxylin and eosin (HE) histopathology is widely available yet lacks direct phenotypic resolution, as highlighted in our recent FineST work[3], motivating computational approaches to infer spatial phenotypes from histological images. In this study, we propose Hist2Pheno (Fig. 1B), a computational framework that enables transforming histological images to nuclei-resolved phenotype predictions by leveraging histological foundation models. Hist2Pheno first performs cross- modality preprocessing, including image registration between HE and PCF imaging data, then expert- XâˆˆRnÃ—p (Fig. 1B, Step 1). Based on these features, Hist2Pheno employs an MLP backbone with a dual- head design to jointly predict fine- and coarse-grained phenotypes, with hierarchical constraints to ensure consistency across levels (Fig. 1B, Step 2). Finally, the trained model generalizes to in-sample and out-of- sample HE images to predict nuclei-resolved phenotype maps at whole-slide scale without requiring additional molecular profiling (Fig. 1B, Step 3). To evaluate the performance of Hist2Pheno in cell-type prediction, we assembled a large-scale dataset of human esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC; Fig. 1A), comprising paired HE and PCF images across multiple clinical conditions, including untreated (SA) and treated cohorts (NCRT, NICT and NCT), totaling over 8 million annotated cells with hierarchical cell-type compositions. Taking the SA and NCRT datasets as a case study (Fig. 1D), we first assessed nuclei segmentation quality and observed strong concordance with CODEX across 28 tumor ROIs (Pearson r = 0.887 for SA and r = 0.898 for NCRT), with mean cell areas (56.94 Î¼m2) consistent with the predefined patch size (8Ã—8 Î¼m2). We then evaluated prediction accuracy by comparing inferred cell types with CODEX annotations from clinical experts on NCRT tumor1 ROI, where Hist2Pheno achieved high accuracy across ROIs (e.g., Acc = 0.832 for Tumors, 0.787 for Epithelial). Visually, from local to global, predicted phenotype maps closely matched the ground truth across all 28 tumor regions under NCRT treatment. Furthermore, we assessed generalization on external datasets and found that Hist2Pheno accurately predicts nuclei-resolved cell types from HE images in a VisiumHD lung dataset, consistent with its adjacent Xenium ground truth. As a computational toolbox, Hist2Pheno enables refined cell-type annotation to provide insights into context-specific cellular interactions and signaling (Fig. 1C), including accurate single-cell mapping, spatial cell-state transitions, and intercellular communication networks. The results on the ESCC dataset are illustrated in Fig. 1D, where we show that the predicted phenotypes achieve strong performance in annotating fine-grained cell types and reveal spatial cell-cell interaction patterns across tumor regions. In particular, Hist2Pheno captures heterogeneous cellular compositions and delineates spatial niches that are consistent with CODEX measurements. Moreover, it extends phenotypic prediction to regions where only histological images are available, enabling whole-slide, nuclei- resolved mapping without additional molecular profiling. This computational framework significantly reduces experimental cost and complexity, offering strong potential for scalable analysis of histopathology images in both research and clinical settings. [We aim to have the full manuscript available as a bioRxiv preprint before the conference.]",
