      "track": "Function: Gene and Protein Function Annotation",
      "title": "Enhancing Protein Function Annotation through Explainable Reasoning with LLM",
      "presenter": "Huiying Yan",
      "format": "Virtual",
      "authors": "Huiying Yan, Hancheng Liu, Shanfeng Zhu",
      "abstract": "Automated protein function prediction/annotation (AFP) is essential for bridging the gap between the exponential growth of protein sequences and very limited experimental knowledge. Recent text-based methods, such as GORetriever and GOAnnotator, have demonstrated that scientific literature provides rich and effective signals for protein function annotation. However, they function as \"black boxes\" that lack interpretability, which prevents them from effectively assisting human experts in manual curation. We present GOReasoner, a framework built on the \"Initiate-Reason-Propagate\" paradigm that employs a fine- tuned LLM, GOExplainer, to enhance in-depth functional reasoning for existing AFP methods. It integrates weighted direct preference optimization(W-DPO) for precise judgment scoring and a Dynamic Propagation (DPP) mechanism to ensure hierarchical consistency. This enables GOReasoner to effectively refine functional candidates derived from different AFP methods. Experiments across multiple benchmarks confirm GOReasonerâ€™s versatility and strong performance across diverse literature sources, from expert annotated to automatically retrieved. By offering verifiable biological explanations and reducing github.com/ZhuLab-Fudan/GOReasoner.",
      "dateLabel": "2026-07-13",
      "startLabel": "12:20",
      "endLabel": "12:40",
      "dayKey": "2026-07-13",
      "searchText": "2026-07-13 12:20:00 12:40:00 lincoln east function: gene and protein function annotation enhancing protein function annotation through explainable reasoning with llm huiying yan huiying yan, hancheng liu, shanfeng zhu automated protein function prediction/annotation (afp) is essential for bridging the gap between the exponential growth of protein sequences and very limited experimental knowledge. recent text-based methods, such as goretriever and goannotator, have demonstrated that scientific literature provides rich and effective signals for protein function annotation. however, they function as \"black boxes\" that lack interpretability, which prevents them from effectively assisting human experts in manual curation. we present goreasoner, a framework built on the \"initiate-reason-propagate\" paradigm that employs a fine- tuned llm, goexplainer, to enhance in-depth functional reasoning for existing afp methods. it integrates weighted direct preference optimization(w-dpo) for precise judgment scoring and a dynamic propagation (dpp) mechanism to ensure hierarchical consistency. this enables goreasoner to effectively refine functional candidates derived from different afp methods. experiments across multiple benchmarks confirm goreasonerâ€™s versatility and strong performance across diverse literature sources, from expert annotated to automatically retrieved. by offering verifiable biological explanations and reducing github.com/zhulab-fudan/goreasoner."
