      "room": "Lincoln West",
      "track": "BOKR: Bio- Ontologies and Knowledge Representation",
      "title": "Constraining Pathogenetic State Hypotheses in Synovial Sarcoma: A Neuro Symbolic Pipeline for SS18::SSX1 Characterization via TTT Linear and Z3 Based SMT Verification",
      "presenter": "George Anstey",
      "format": "Virtual",
      "authors": "George Anstey, Noel Anstey",
      "abstract": "We introduce a neuro-symbolic pipeline for characterizing system level failures driven by the SS18::SSX1 fusion protein in synovial sarcoma. Characterizing this pathogenesis requires bridging molecular level protein disruptions with phenotypic observables a task that challenges standard deep learning models due to the dual requirements of long range sequence dependency and formal causal consistency. The proposed architecture implements an operational interface between test time training (TTT) architectures and satisfiability modulo theories (SMT) based verification using Z3. Within this pipeline a TTT Linear model processes protein sequence data to propose candidate state transition sequences. These sequences are mapped into symbolic predicates and subjected to formal verification against a knowledge base of encoded biological rules. Z3 functions as a constraint-guided pruning layer rejecting candidates that violate consistency constraints involving phenotypic markers such as LDH and CD56. Importantly, Z3 does not merely validate candidate predictions it constrains the admissible hypothesis space generated by TTT Linear outputs. This hierarchical methodology produces reconstructions that are computationally expressive and biologically auditable. Preliminary experiments indicate that this constrained approach reduces the proposal of biologically incoherent states relative to unconstrained sequence modeling baselines.",
      "dateLabel": "2026-07-13",
      "startLabel": "12:40",
      "endLabel": "13:00",
      "dayKey": "2026-07-13",
      "searchText": "2026-07-13 12:40:00 13:00:00 lincoln west bokr: bio- ontologies and knowledge representation constraining pathogenetic state hypotheses in synovial sarcoma: a neuro symbolic pipeline for ss18::ssx1 characterization via ttt linear and z3 based smt verification george anstey george anstey, noel anstey we introduce a neuro-symbolic pipeline for characterizing system level failures driven by the ss18::ssx1 fusion protein in synovial sarcoma. characterizing this pathogenesis requires bridging molecular level protein disruptions with phenotypic observables a task that challenges standard deep learning models due to the dual requirements of long range sequence dependency and formal causal consistency. the proposed architecture implements an operational interface between test time training (ttt) architectures and satisfiability modulo theories (smt) based verification using z3. within this pipeline a ttt linear model processes protein sequence data to propose candidate state transition sequences. these sequences are mapped into symbolic predicates and subjected to formal verification against a knowledge base of encoded biological rules. z3 functions as a constraint-guided pruning layer rejecting candidates that violate consistency constraints involving phenotypic markers such as ldh and cd56. importantly, z3 does not merely validate candidate predictions it constrains the admissible hypothesis space generated by ttt linear outputs. this hierarchical methodology produces reconstructions that are computationally expressive and biologically auditable. preliminary experiments indicate that this constrained approach reduces the proposal of biologically incoherent states relative to unconstrained sequence modeling baselines."
