      "startLabel": "15:40",
      "endLabel": "16:00",
      "dayKey": "2026-07-13",
      "searchText": "2026-07-13 15:40:00 16:00:00 jefferson east csi: computational systems immunology scalable unmutated common ancestor inference of paired b cell receptor ancestors across immune repertoires cole jensen cole jensen, steven h. kleinstein, kenneth hoehn b cells play a central role in adaptive immunity by producing antibodies that recognize and neutralize pathogens. each b cell expresses a unique b cell receptor (bcr) composed of paired heavy and light chains generated through v(d)j recombination, creating a diverse repertoire of antigen-inexperienced (naive) b cells capable of recognizing a wide range of antigens. during immune responses, naive b cells become activated, proliferate, and diversify through somatic hypermutation and selection, generating related descendants whose evolutionary relationships can be reconstructed using phylogenetic methods. these lineage trees illuminate affinity maturation trajectories, key mutations, and antibody evolution. a key goal is inferring unobserved ancestors, including the naive unmutated common ancestor (uca). however, accurate uca inference is challenging because phylogenetic methods alone do not fully account for diversification processes shaping bcr sequences, specifically those imposed by v(d)j recombination. comparable or improved accuracy and faster runtimes than existing methods. it uniquely infers paired heavy-light chain ucas from single-cell data. we present two complementary analyses: paired reconstruction of ucas and intermediates for a broadly neutralizing antibodies hiv lineage, and identification of shared mutations across entire bcr sequences in convergent clones. scalable reconstruction of lineage origins allows repertoire-wide study of mutations in critical antibody regions. this capability will strengthen the interpretation of immune repertoire data and accelerate progress in vaccine design, antibody engineering, and immunotherapy. our method is implemented in the r package dowser."
