      "format": "Virtual",
      "authors": "Zhao Li, Yue Qi, Shaosen Zhang, Zhang Zhang, Lina Ma Aufgebauer, Paul Collier, Jacqueline Proszynski, Krista Ryon, Evan Violette, Nadia Houerbi, Stacy Horner, Christopher Mason",
      "abstract": "Chromatin-associated long non-coding RNAs (ca-lncRNAs) play crucial roles in genome organization and gene regulation, yet systematic identification and comprehensive characterization of human ca-lncRNAs remain inadequate. Here, we identified human ca-lncRNAs genome-wide using high-throughput sequencing datasets that assess RNA-chromatin association, and comprehensively characterized their multi-dimensional molecular features via integrative multi-omics analysis. Across six cell lines, we identified 17,875 high-confidence ca-lncRNAs among 323,950 lncRNAs from LncBook. These constitute nearly 80% of all detected chromatin-associated RNAs and display significantly higher cell-type specificity. Multi-omics analysis across more than 1,000 samples indicates that, compared with chromatin- disassociated lncRNAs, ca-lncRNAs exhibit significantly higher expression levels, lower protein-coding potential, and more frequent interactions with RNA-binding proteins such as hnRNPs and transcription factors. Notably, ca-lncRNA genes are generally young and predominantly originate in the Eutheria and Homo sapiens-specific lineages. To investigate the sequence basis for chromatin binding, we applied an interpretable machine learning framework and revealed that Alu repeats are the key drivers of chromatin binding. Mechanistically, integrative interactome analysis combined with computational prediction suggested that Alu repeats facilitate RNA-chromatin interactions through Alu-DNA triplex formation. Collectively, our study provides a comprehensive resource of human ca-lncRNAs, delineates their multi- omics characteristics, and highlights Alu repeats as key sequence elements of chromatin binding. workflows typically operate at the gene level or rely on predefined reference transcriptomes, limiting resolution of isoform-specific RNA modification patterns. Here, we introduce modulator, a unified pipeline for transcript-aware epitranscriptomic analysis that operates on fragmentforms: read-supported, 3prime- anchored RNA structures defined by shared splice junctions and transcript end sites (TES). Unlike canonical transcripts, which represent full-length isoforms often derived from reference annotations, fragmentforms are data-driven partial isoform representations that prioritize reliable features of Nanopore DRS reads, namely splice structure and poly(A)-anchored ends, while accommodating incomplete or variable coverage. This distinction enables robust modeling of RNA populations in contexts where full- length transcript reconstruction is ambiguous or unsupported. modulator reconstructs fragmentforms directly from aligned reads using TES clustering and splice-chain collapsing, and assigns reads to these structures using a 3prime-anchored suffix-based strategy. Transcript identity is encoded in BAM tags and used to partition modification calls generated by ONT modkit, yielding fragmentform-specific modification profiles across multiple RNA modification types. Downstream modules aggregate genotype and modification signals across samples, apply site-level quality filtering, and perform differential analysis. Across experimental datasets, modulator reveals widespread fragmentform-specific differences in RNA modification stoichiometry, including substantial heterogeneity among structures derived from the same gene and condition-specific modulation of these patterns. These results demonstrate that fragmentform- level resolution is critical for disentangling RNA processing from epitranscriptomic regulation. modulator provides a reproducible framework for integrative RNA biology, enabling sample-specific transcript- resolved modification analysis without reliance on incomplete reference annotations. Availability: https:// github.com/Theo-Nelson/modulator Acknowledgements: T.M.N. was supported by a Medical Scientist Training Program grant from the National Institute of General Medical Sciences of the National Institutes of Health under award number: T32GM152349 to the Weill Cornell/Rockefeller/Sloan Kettering Tri- Institutional MD-PhD Program. T.M.N. was also supported with computational resources from the National Science Foundation ACCESS Allocation Request BIO240371. The computations by T.M.N. in this paper were run with additional HPC resources supported by the Scientific Computing Unit at Weill Cornell Medicine. Research on m6A in the Horner lab has been supported by Burroughs Wellcome Fund and National Institutes of Health (NIH) grant R01AI125416. Additionally, this material is based upon work supported by the National Science Foundation Graduate Research Fellowship (C.J.A), and the CMB training grant T32GM142605 (C.J.A). C.E.M. was supported by the Katherine And John Bleckman Foundation, and the NIH (U54AG089334, R01AI125416). E.M.V. was supported by the Weill Cornell ACCESS Summer Internship Program.",
