      "abstract": "Today, there are millions of samples from hundreds of thousands of studies in publicly available repositories like the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) [1] and the Sequence Read Archive (SRA) [2]. These data are an invaluable resource to reuse to investigate novel biomedical questions. However, a key barrier to reusing these data is the poor quality of their metadata. Almost all public metadata are in the form of unstructured plain text using varied terminologies and including extraneous pieces of information. To overcome this barrier, many research groups have independently manually curated annotation sets for public samples and studies, linking them to terms in controlled vocabularies. However, these resources are scattered across online resources in web servers, GitHub repositories, data sharing resources like Zenodo, or supplemental files in publications. Additionally, these resources store annotations in various formats and use a diverse set of controlled vocabularies. To combine annotations across these resources to use at-scale would require an incredible amount of work to harmonize all of the annotation schemas. To that end, we built MetaHQ, a platform to harmonize and distribute standardized tissue, disease, sex, and age annotations for over 188,000 transcriptomics samples and 11,000 studies queryable through a command line interface (CLI) [3] (Figure 1). MetaHQ contains a collection of annotations harmonized across 13 independent sources harmonized into a single database with a standardized annotation schema. Users can use our CLI to seamlessly query the database and retrieve annotations for samples or studies, filter for and what is unknown. These labels explicitly define nuances in MetaHQ annotations in formats ready for machine learning (ML) classification. Lastly, we include references to the original sources of all annotations in all MetaHQ outputs, along with additional documentation and guidance, to enable users to cite the original sources if they use annotations in MetaHQ. Figure 1. (A) The MetaHQ harmonization and standardization process. (B) The number of annotations in MetaHQ across attributes and the number of unique tissue and disease terms represented. (C) The MetaHQ CLI pipeline. MetaHQ is, to our knowledge, the largest collection of standardized tissue, disease, sex, and age annotations. Yet, there are still millions of samples and hundreds of thousands of studies left without standardized annotations. With the exponential growth of public databases like GEO and SRA, manually annotating the remaining samples and studies is, realistically, an impossible feat. Therefore, scalable natural language processing (NLP) methods are essential for inferring standardized annotations of samples and studies directly from their unstructured plain text metadata records. Numerous NLP approaches have been developed to extract contextual information from biomedical texts [4â€“7]. However, none of these approaches offer all four of the following qualities: interpretability, scalability, predictions to standardized terms, and generalizability. Each method has its strengths with regards to one or two of these qualities, however none possess all. For these reasons, we developed an interpretable, generalizable, and lightweight text-based ML approach, txt2onto [8], to predict standardized tissue, cell type, and disease annotations from biomedical metadata. To maximize the accessibility of our text-based predictions, we developed Meta2Onto, a web server to search for standardized annotations for hundreds of thousands of studies in GEO. Meta2Onto currently stores our predicted tissue and disease annotations for over 270,000 GEO studies (Figure 2). Users can filter results for various study attributes such as technology, platform, study sample size, and annotation confidence. Users can also filter by whether the data from the study is available in large, ready-to-use public expression compendia [9â€“11]. Transparency and interpretability are at the core of Meta2Onto. Our predictions are highly interpretable where words/phrases that contributed to each prediction are highlighted for each retrieved study. To maximize transparency, we enable users to calibrate their trust based on the performance of our text-based models for the userâ€™s tissue or disease of interest, which we show prominently. Also displayed for each retrieved study is the confidence our model has for that particular annotation. Lastly, we leverage the facts that users can easily read a metadata record and the words/phrases therein that contributed to the prediction to provide prediction-level feedback functionalities. Using these functionalities, researchers can provide high-level and detailed feedback on various attributes of prediction quality for individual studies. Together, MetaHQ and Meta2Onto offer both manually-curated and text-predicted standardized annotations, respectively. Through these resources, we strive to increase the findability, accessibility, interoperability, and reusability of public omics data to drive novel biomedical hypothesis generation and discovery at-scale. REFERENCES 1. Clough E, Barrett T, Wilhite SE, Ledoux P, Evangelista C, Kim IF, et al. NCBI GEO: archive for gene expression and epigenomics data sets: 23-year update. Nucleic Acids Res. 2024;52: D138â€“D144. 2. Katz K, Shutov O, Lapoint R, Kimelman M, Brister JR, Oâ€™Sullivan C. The Sequence Read Archive: a decade more of explosive growth. Nucleic Acids Res. 2022;50: D387â€“D390. 3. Hicks P, Valtadoros LE, Mancuso CA, Alquadoomi F, Johnson KA, Sundar S, et al. MetaHQ: Harmonized, high-quality metadata annotations of public omics samples and studies. arXiv [q-bio.GN]. 2026. doi:10.48550/arXiv.2602.07805 4. Serna Garcia G, Leone M, Bernasconi A, Carman MJ. GeMI: interactive interface for transformer-based Genomic Metadata Integration. Database (Oxford). 2022;2022. doi:10.1093/database/baac036 5. Hawkins NT, Maldaver M, Yannakopoulos A, Guare LA, Krishnan A. Systematic tissue annotations of genomics samples by modeling unstructured metadata. Nat Commun. 2022;13: 6736. 6. Bernstein MN, Doan A, Dewey CN. MetaSRA: normalized human sample-specific metadata for the Sequence Read Archive. Bioinformatics. 2017;33: 2914â€“2923. 7. Wang Z, Monteiro CD, Jagodnik KM, Fernandez NF, Gundersen GW, Rouillard AD, et al. Extraction and analysis of signatures from the Gene Expression Omnibus by the crowd. Nat Commun. 2016;7: 12846. 8. Yuan H, Hicks P, Ahmadian M, Johnson KA, Valtadoros L, Krishnan A. Annotating publicly-available samples and studies using interpretable modeling of unstructured metadata. Brief Bioinform. 2024;26: bbae652. 9. Lachmann A, Torre D, Keenan AB, Jagodnik KM, Lee HJ, Wang L, et al. Massive mining of publicly available RNA-seq data from human and mouse. Nat Commun. 2018;9: 1366. 10. Greene CS, Hu D, Jones RWW, Liu S, Mejia DS. refine. bio: A resource of uniformly processed publicly available gene expression datasets. Google Scholar. 2023. 11. Wilks C, Zheng SC, Chen FY, Charles R, Solomon B, Ling JP, et al. recount3: summaries and queries for large-scale RNA-seq expression and splicing. Genome Biol. 2021;22: 323.",
