      "title": "KGXVal: Open-source quality control and curation support for biomedical",
      "presenter": "Daniel Korn",
      "format": "In person",
      "authors": "Daniel Korn, Matthew Brush, Kevin Schaper, Melissa Haendel",
      "abstract": "The Knowledge Graph Exchange (KGX) data standard eases harmonization of biomedical data in the creation of knowledge graphs across diverse research domains (e.g. pharmacology, genomics, and proteomics). Existing data sources can be mapped onto the KGX specification and normalized against shared ontologies, supporting interoperable, multi-domain knowledge graphs. However, these conversion flexibility to cleanly represent all biomedical modalities. Manual review of KGX files for such inconsistencies has historically required specialized tooling and considerable expert effort. We present KGXVal, a Python tool that automates this validation step and surfaces errors directly to human curators. KGXVal accepts KGX-formatted files as input and produces three categories of output: (1) summary statistics on the frequency and distribution of unique metalabel combinations, (2) stratified samples across each combination type for targeted review, and (3) a complete enumeration of KGX model violations detected before and after normalization. All outputs are written as XLSX and CSV files, enabling immediate inspection in standard spreadsheet applications without additional tooling. In an initial analysis of 26 data sources harmonized to KGX format, KGXVal identified over 3100 instances of KGX schema errors, demonstrating the scale of quality issues that previously went undetected. KGXVal benefits from an ecosystem of existing open-source tools for working with KGX and biomedical data and is released under the BSD-3-Clause license and is available at https://github.com/monarch-initiative/kgxval.",
