      "title": "scPTR: Decomposing Post-Transcriptional Regulation at Single- Cell Resolution",
      "presenter": "Bryan Cheng",
      "format": "In person",
      "authors": "Bryan Cheng, Austin Jin",
      "abstract": "Transcript abundance is jointly determined by synthesis and degradation, yet single-cell computational frameworks address only transcriptional dynamics. RNA velocity models mRNA accumulation from spliced/unspliced ratios, but mRNA degradationâ€”orchestrated by RNA-binding proteins (RBPs), microRNAs, and cis-regulatory elementsâ€”is an equally fundamental determinant of cell state and a recognized driver of fate transitions. No framework exists to estimate per-cell degradation rates, discover populations defined by post-transcriptional programs, or infer mRNA stability networks at single-cell resolution. We present scPTR (Single-Cell Post-Transcriptional Regulatory Decomposition), which estimates per-cell, per-gene mRNA degradation rates (Î³ = Î²Â·u/s) from standard scRNA-seq. On 10x developmental data, scPTR (Spearman Ï\u0000= âˆ’0.40 vs. published half-lives) outperforms scVelo steady- state (Ï\u0000= âˆ’0.37) and velVI (Ï\u0000= âˆ’0.28); in sci-fate metabolic-labeling data, Ï\u0000r eaches âˆ’0.81. Estimates correlate with 3â€² UTR length (Ï\u0000= 0.34, p < 10â\u0000»Â²â\u0000°â\u0000a°n)d AU content (Ï\u0000= 0.30, p < 10â\u0000»Â¹â\u0000µâ\u0000°), and 59% of 215 miRNA families show significant target enrichment (aggregate p = 4.7Ã—10â\u0000»â\u0000¶â\u0000µ). for tissue-appropriate pathways (ER stress/autophagy; synaptic plasticity). Post-transcriptional velocity shows that degradation-rate changes precede expression changes for 54% of transition genes in pancreas (p < 10â\u0000»â\u0000µâ\u0000a·n)d 78% in dentate gyrus (p = 9.9Ã—10â\u0000»Â¹Â³).R BP network inference identifies functionally essential hub regulators (DepMap CRISPR; p = 6.4Ã—10â\u0000»â\u0000µa)n d reveals oncogenic mRNA stabilization in neuroblastoma (66% stabilizing edges). scPTR opens the post-transcriptional layer of single-cell biology to systematic, quantitative investigation",
