      "title": "A Unified Substitution– Mutation Rate Ratio (c/µ) Framework and Near-Neutral Selectionist Theory for Molecular Evolution: Insights from SARS-CoV-2",
      "presenter": "Chun Wu",
      "format": "In person",
      "authors": "Chun Wu, Nicholas Paradis, Khushi Jain",
      "abstract": "Understanding how mutations shape genome evolution remains a central challenge, particularly in resolving the neutralistâ€“selectionist debate. Traditional frameworks such as Ka/Ks and mutationâ€“selection (MutSel) models provide important insights but have key limitations. Ka/Ks assumes synonymous mutations are neutral and is restricted to coding regions, while MutSel requires equilibrium assumptions and significant computational complexity, limiting genome-wide applicability, especially in untranslated regions (UTRs). To overcome these limitations, we introduce a unified framework based on the substitutionâ€“mutation rate ratio (c/Âµ), which quantifies selection at the nucleotide level across entire genomes. Here, c represents the substitution rate and Âµ the mutation rate. The ratio c/Âµ directly measures fitness effects, classifying mutations as beneficial (c/Âµ > 1), neutral (c/Âµ = 1), or deleterious (c/Âµ < 1), without assuming synonymous neutrality and with applicability to both translated and untranslated regions. We further derive the relationship c/Âµ = Ps(Ks/Âµ) + Pa(Ka/Âµ), showing that Ka/ Ks is equivalent to c/Âµ only when synonymous mutations are neutral. Empirical SARS-CoV-2 analysis demonstrates frequent violations of this assumption, leading to misclassification by Ka/Ks. Building on this, we develop the Near-Neutral Selectionist Theory (NNST), supported by L-shaped distributions of fitness effects dominated by deleterious mutations with smaller fractions of neutral and beneficial mutations. Two regimes emerge: Near-Neutral Balanced Selectionist Theory (NNBST), explaining strict molecular clocks through balanced near-neutral effects, and Near-Neutral Unbalanced Selectionist Theory (NNUST), explaining deviations from clock behavior. Analysis of 49 genomic segments shows both regimes coexist, indicating evolution is governed by a spectrum of near-neutral dynamics. Together, c/Âµ and NNST provide a unified, data-driven framework that reconciles neutral and selectionist perspectives, offering a more complete model of genome evolution.",
      "dateLabel": "2026-07-16",
      "startLabel": "15:00",
      "endLabel": "15:20",
      "dayKey": "2026-07-16",
      "searchText": "2026-07-16 15:00:00 15:20:00 jefferson west evolcompgen: evolution & comparative genomics a unified substitution– mutation rate ratio (c/µ) framework and near-neutral selectionist theory for molecular evolution: insights from sars-cov-2 chun wu chun wu, nicholas paradis, khushi jain understanding how mutations shape genome evolution remains a central challenge, particularly in resolving the neutralistâ€“selectionist debate. traditional frameworks such as ka/ks and mutationâ€“selection (mutsel) models provide important insights but have key limitations. ka/ks assumes synonymous mutations are neutral and is restricted to coding regions, while mutsel requires equilibrium assumptions and significant computational complexity, limiting genome-wide applicability, especially in untranslated regions (utrs). to overcome these limitations, we introduce a unified framework based on the substitutionâ€“mutation rate ratio (c/âµ), which quantifies selection at the nucleotide level across entire genomes. here, c represents the substitution rate and âµ the mutation rate. the ratio c/âµ directly measures fitness effects, classifying mutations as beneficial (c/âµ > 1), neutral (c/âµ = 1), or deleterious (c/âµ < 1), without assuming synonymous neutrality and with applicability to both translated and untranslated regions. we further derive the relationship c/âµ = ps(ks/âµ) + pa(ka/âµ), showing that ka/ ks is equivalent to c/âµ only when synonymous mutations are neutral. empirical sars-cov-2 analysis demonstrates frequent violations of this assumption, leading to misclassification by ka/ks. building on this, we develop the near-neutral selectionist theory (nnst), supported by l-shaped distributions of fitness effects dominated by deleterious mutations with smaller fractions of neutral and beneficial mutations. two regimes emerge: near-neutral balanced selectionist theory (nnbst), explaining strict molecular clocks through balanced near-neutral effects, and near-neutral unbalanced selectionist theory (nnust), explaining deviations from clock behavior. analysis of 49 genomic segments shows both regimes coexist, indicating evolution is governed by a spectrum of near-neutral dynamics. together, c/âµ and nnst provide a unified, data-driven framework that reconciles neutral and selectionist perspectives, offering a more complete model of genome evolution."
