      "title": "Adapting Mamba 2 For EHR Data Using Continual Learning",
      "presenter": "Conrad Testagrose",
      "format": "In person",
      "authors": "Conrad Testagrose, Simone Rancati, Micheal Sy, Riccardo Bellazzi, Ziyi Chen, Cheng Peng, Simone Marini, Marco Salemi, Christina Boucher, Mattia Prosperi",
      "abstract": "Clinical language models have demonstrated the value of pretraining on electronic health records (EHRs), but most prior clinical transformers are limited to context windows of 512 to 8,192 tokens. Full hospital admissions linearized with laboratory events, medications, transfers, and clinical notes frequently exceed these limits. State space models (SSMs) such as Mamba 2 offer a promising alternative due to their linear scaling with sequence length, but whether their recurrent state dynamics can maintain coherent representations across very long clinical sequences has not been established. We present a continually pretrained Mamba 2 2.7B model trained at 131,072 token context on structured linearizations of 383,222 MIMIC-IV hospital admissions, using a blend of medical text and EHR data. Through a sequence length ablation spanning 1,024 to 131,072 tokens, we characterize how base and continually pretrained SSMs respond to increasing context length. The base model performs reasonably at short context (NLL) of 1.58 at 1,024 tokens) but degrades beyond its training range, reaching NLL 12.14 and perplexity 187,543 at 131,072 tokens. The continually pretrained model exhibits the opposite trend, with NLL decreasing from 0.62 to 0.48 across the same range. Linear probing for discharge disposition prediction confirms that these gains translate to downstream clinical utility, the pretrained model improves monotonically from AUROC 0.803 at 25% of admission to 0.844 at full admission, while the base model declines from 0.776 to",
      "dateLabel": "2026-07-16",
      "startLabel": "15:00",
      "endLabel": "15:20",
